# Adventure map AI — `NWorld::CPlayerAI`

First survey (branch `advmap-ai`). Most of the CPlayerAI code (`0x00866000`–`0x00871000`) is **not defined as functions in
Ghidra** (only reached through vtables / jump tables): decompile fails there until functions are created in Ghidra.
Use `dumpbin -disasm -range:start,end H5X.exe` (VS 18 toolset, `14.51.x`) meanwhile.

## Problems to fix (design intent)

The AI ignores much of what a real player does on the adventure map:
- It does not pick up resources and artifacts after defeating the monsters guarding them.
- It does not capture map buildings (mines, dwellings, …).
- It does not clear the neutral stacks around its starting town.
- It clears only a few monsters to level up early, then rushes an enemy hero. With its difficulty bonuses (extra
  resources, ignored creature losses in battle, extra creature growth) it is often unbeatable when it comes early.

Goal: the AI should spend more time exploring and collecting everything it can before attacking. That is why H5X sets
the `RPGStats/AI/Adventure` parameters as it does (lower aggressivity, higher cowardice, no avenger vs computer, …):
the parameters alone do not achieve it, so the fix has to come from the exe (choice building / estimation / destination
search).

## Classes (RTTI)

| Class | Notes |
|---|---|
| `NWorld::CPlayerAI` (`IPlayerAI`) | One per AI player. Main vtable `0x00E4C8BC` (4 slots), second vtable `0x00E4C89C` at subobject `+0x13C` |
| `NWorld::CPlayerAIInformation` | Per-player AI info |
| `NWorld::CDangerPool` (`IDangerPool`) | Threat bookkeeping, vtables `0x00E8AB0C` / `0x00E8AAEC` |
| `NWorld::CStrategicGoals` / `Group` / `Table` | Strategic goals, vtables `0x00E8A084` (12 slots) … |
| `NWorld::CConquestPlanForProvince` | vtable `0x00E71860` |
| `NWorld::CAICmds`, `CAIArrangeTownHeroesCmd`, `CAIPurchaseCreaturesInTown`, `CAIResolveJoiningArmiesCmd`, `CAIRevisitTownCmd`, `CAISolvePuzzleMapCmd` | AI world commands |
| `NAI::*` | 3D/AI geometry (convex hulls, octree) — **not** the adventure AI |

These vtables only hold lifetime/serialization methods; the decision logic is non-virtual.

## `CPlayerAI` layout (partial)

| Offset | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `+0x08` | player |
| `+0x0C` | world / game object (vtable `+0xA8` → current day) |
| `+0x10` | step counter this turn (printed by "end turn: %i steps") |
| `+0x20` | `timeToWork` (last day the AI ran) |
| `+0x64` | per-day job bit mask, reset by `SFLB_InitDay` (`(x & 0xFFFE739C) | 0xE739C`) |
| `+0x128` | **current step** (enum below) |
| `+0x12C` | tick count at turn start |

## Turn state machine

`CPlayerAI` main vtable slot 0 = `0x0086DD40`. Its dispatcher at `0x0086DF40`: `ebx = [esi+0x128]`, `cmp ebx,0xE; ja`,
`jmp [ebx*4 + 0x0086E14C]`. Step names from `FUN_00866550`; slot 3 (`0x00866CF0`) returns the progress-bar value per step.

| # | Step | Handler | Main external callees |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `INIT_DAY` | `0x00867440` (`CPlayerAI::SFLB_InitDay`, exported by H5X.exe) then `0x0086D750` | |
| 1 | `MOVE_HERO` | `0x0086B400` | `0x004B94B0`, `0x0068BD20`, `0x006BD7C0` |
| 2 | `COLLECT_PASSABLE_OBJECTS` | `0x00867520` | `0x004F6770`, `0x00C27740`, `0x00872620` |
| 3 | `BUILD_HERO_CHOICES` | `0x0086C760` | `0x00BD7110`, `0x00A37050` |
| 4 | `ESTIMATE_CHOICES` | `0x008667D0` | **`0x00872000`** (choice estimator) |
| 5 | `EXECUTE_BEST_CHOICE` | `0x0086C6B0` | `0x004E6C60`, `0x004E9A00` |
| 6 | `MANAGE_TOWNS` | `0x0086D4E0` | `0x00C283D0`, `0x009C5C60` |
| 7 | `HIRE_HEROES` | `0x0086B0A0` | `0x0098DE60`, `0x00C02A90` |
| 8 | `LAST_WAIT` | `0x00866700` | sets step 9 |
| 9 | `RETURN_TURN` | `0x00868EB0` | logs "end turn: %i steps, %i.%i seconds", ends the turn |
| 10 | `START_CHOOSE_DESTINATION` | `0x0086C570` | `0x00C27740`, `0x00872620`, `0x0063EE50` |
| 11 | `CHOOSE_DESTINATION` | `0x0086C010` | `0x00C07100`, `0x00C076D0`, `0x00C07AD0`, `0x00C07B20`, `0x00BCC020` |
| 12 | `MANAGE_HEROES` | `0x00869AD0` | `0x00BD7110`, `0x0074E870`, `0x00C5AF30` |
| 13 | `STEP_REARRANGE_TOWN_HEROES` | `0x00868CA0` | `0x0068BD20` |
| 14 | `STEP_ORDER_CARAVANS` | `0x0086A350` | `0x00CE6110`, `0x00CEA190` |

Step writes (`mov [reg+0x128], n`) are spread over the handlers above; `0x00868E00` (called after `SFLB_InitDay`)
sets the first step of the day.

